  "notes" : [
    {
      "note" : "# Welcome to Fabric\n\nTheres a lot of ground to cover, but dont worry we’ll take it one step at a time!\n\n### The Interface:\n\nThe Fabric Editor consists of 4 major areas:\n\n1. The ***Canvas*** - the centeral part of the UI you are currentlty looking at, where ***Nodes*** and ***Notes*** (like this one) live.\n2. The ***Sidebar*** - this is a place where available ***Nodes*** are organized and searchable. You can toggle the sidebar from the toolbars top left button.\n3. The ***Inspector*** - this is the rightmost sidebar (and similarly toggle-able via the toolbars right most button), and allows you to inspect and adjust a selected nodes values, as well as interact with published values. \n4. The ***Viewer*** - this secondary window is a place to view the visual output of your scene. You can adjust the viewers size or place it full screen on a secondary display.\n\n\nYou can navigate the canvas by swiping left and right, and use pinch gestures to zoom in and out.\n\n\n### Fabric Basics:\n\nIn Fabric, you use a library of Nodes connected to one another to form a graph and do something useful, interesting or fun.\n\nYou can think of a node like a function, which takes some input, does work and produces output, and a graph like a set of functions that work together to produce some aggregate result.\n\nA node may have multiple input and output ***Ports*** which are color coded to reflect their data type. \n\nGenerally, only inputs and outputs of the same data type can be connected.\n\nWithout going too deep into the details, parameters are grey - and consist of numbers  or sets of numbers, and String values. Colored ports represent objects like Images, Geometry, Materials, and so forth. \n\n[You can learn more about Nodes and Data Types in our Architecture doc](https:\/\/github.com\/Fabric-Project\/Fabric\/blob\/main\/ARCHITECTURE.md)\n\n[You can see a list of available nodes on our github repository](https:\/\/github.com\/Fabric-Project\/Fabric\/blob\/main\/NODES.md)\n\n  \n### Experiment!\n\nFabric is meant to be a fun, exploratory experience. Don’t hesitate, click around, connect stuff up and learn by doing!",
      "rect" : [
